THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

EuroOS is written from scratch (its own kernel, filesystem, network stack,
crypto integration layer, and desktop). It builds on a small set of permissively
licensed Rust crates, each retaining its own licence. The EUPL covers the EuroOS
source; the components below remain under their respective licences:

  Component                         Licence              Use in EuroOS
  --------------------------------  -------------------  ---------------------------
  RustCrypto: sha2, poly1305,       MIT OR Apache-2.0    hashing / AEAD primitives
    chacha20, chacha20poly1305,                          (forced soft backends)
    digest, cpufeatures, subtle
  ed25519-dalek, x25519-dalek,      MIT OR Apache-2.0    signatures / key exchange
    curve25519-dalek                                     (serial backend)
  ab_glyph (+ libm)                 Apache-2.0 / MIT     desktop font rasterisation
  spin                              MIT                  no_std synchronisation
  linked_list_allocator            Apache-2.0 / MIT      kernel heap
  x86_64                            MIT / Apache-2.0     CPU/paging abstractions
  uefi / uefi-services             MPL-2.0              UEFI boot environment
  Rust core / alloc / compiler-     MIT / Apache-2.0     no_std runtime (build-std)
    builtins

The EUPL v1.2 is explicitly compatible with these permissive licences and with
copyleft licences listed in its Appendix (GPLv2/v3, AGPLv3, MPL-2.0, and others),
allowing EuroOS to be combined and redistributed within the European and wider
open-source legal context.

Full dependency licences are reproduced by `cargo` under each crate; run
`cargo tree` for the complete, version-pinned dependency graph (Cargo.lock is
committed for reproducibility).
